- Children’s and adolescents’ learning with educational technology
1. Time spent, enjoyment, participation, and completion rates are often mistaken for learning.
2. The real test of learning happens after the app is closed. Students may perform well within a tool but fail to retain or apply what they learned elsewhere.
2. Features designed to capture attention can pull attention away from learning.
3. The amount of time spent on screens is not as important as the activity it is replacing.
4. Many claims about improving critical thinking, intelligence, and brain power are not supported by evidence.
5. AI may improve performance while reducing learning. Students can produce better work while developing less independent knowledge and skill.
Source: https://www.apa.org/pubs/reports/children-adolescent-learning-educational-technology

